      ":question/id": "cffd31fb-6e24-58d9-bedb-a4c483fcb431",
      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-120311",
      ":question/problem": "The point $P$ has polar coordinates $(4,{225}^{∘}).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡({225}^{∘}) \\\\  & =4\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡({225}^{∘}) \\\\  & =4\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-2\\sqrt{2},-2\\sqrt{2}).$"
    }
