      ":question/id": "eb68afad-979d-5067-bbdf-a0270d1ebbf5",
      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-276511",
      ":question/problem": "If the point $P$ has polar coordinates $(2,\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ then its Cartesian coordinates are$\\,(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ x & =2\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\ x & =2\\cdot 0 \\\\ x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y & =2\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\ y & =2\\cdot 1 \\\\ y & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(0,2).$"
    }
