      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-276514",
      ":question/problem": "If the point $P$ has polar coordinates $(4,\\frac{7\\pi }{6}),$ then its Cartesian coordinates are\n\n$(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-2\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =4\\cdot -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-2\\sqrt{3},-2).$"
    }
  },
