      ":question/id": "f70a1b51-73cd-5475-aadd-7e2e3bee491f",
      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-8519",
      ":question/problem": "The point $P$ has polar coordinates $(6,{45}^{∘}).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate od $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ x & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡({45}^{∘}) \\\\ x & =6\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ x & =3\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡({45}^{∘}) \\\\ y & =6\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ y & =3\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(3\\sqrt{2},3\\sqrt{2}).$"
