      ":question/id": "34703524-66bb-5912-b698-6c1e0fbc88e2",
      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-17122",
      ":question/problem": "The point $P$ has polar coordinates $(1,\\pi ).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ x & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\ x & =1(-1) \\\\ x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\ y & =1\\cdot 0 \\\\ y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-1,0).$"
    }
