      ":question/id": "c8aac56f-2bf8-5ea3-a6d1-b31fe28b87af",
      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-8517",
      ":question/problem": "The point $P$ has polar coordinates $(10,\\frac{2\\pi }{3}).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ x & =10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\ x & =10\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\ x & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y & =10\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\ y & =10\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ y & =5\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-5,5\\sqrt{3}).$"
    }
  },
