      ":question/id": "8043b0ac-0a10-5e97-957e-d555cd373e68",
      ":question/problem": "The point $P$ has polar coordinates $\\left(4,\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)$. Find the exact Cartesian coordinates of $P$.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, use\n\n$$\nx=r\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=r\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nThe right triangle shows why cosine gives the horizontal component and sine gives the vertical component.\n\n![](images/20/205b3684042b0ab0261a4d56800cd89a10a7816f8022befcf6c664337f834380.png)\n\nHere $r=4$ and $\\theta=\\frac{\\pi}{6}$. First calculate the $x$-coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=4\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)\\\\\n &=4\\cdot\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\\\\n &=2\\sqrt{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen calculate the $y$-coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny&=4\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)\\\\\n &=4\\cdot\\frac{1}{2}\\\\\n &=2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBoth coordinates are positive, as expected in the first quadrant. Therefore, the Cartesian coordinates are $\\left(2\\sqrt{3},2\\right)$."
    }
