      ":question/id": "353cf584-808b-530b-86d4-e0918d9e97cd",
      ":question/problem": "The point $P$ has polar coordinates $\\left(3,\\frac{5\\pi}{6}\\right)$. Find the exact Cartesian coordinates of $P$.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, use\n\n$$\nx=r\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=r\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nThe angle $\\frac{5\\pi}{6}$ lies in the second quadrant and has reference angle $\\frac{\\pi}{6}$. Hence cosine is negative and sine is positive:\n\n$$\n\\cos\\left(\\frac{5\\pi}{6}\\right)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\qquad\n\\sin\\left(\\frac{5\\pi}{6}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst calculate the $x$-coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=3\\cos\\left(\\frac{5\\pi}{6}\\right)\\\\\n &=3\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)\\\\\n &=-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen calculate the $y$-coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny&=3\\sin\\left(\\frac{5\\pi}{6}\\right)\\\\\n &=3\\cdot\\frac{1}{2}\\\\\n &=\\frac{3}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates are $\\left(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{3}{2}\\right)$. The negative $x$ and positive $y$ confirm the second quadrant."
    }
