      ":question/id": "ab27e4c8-0620-540c-9f5a-41d839c0c6f0",
      ":question/problem": "The point $P$ has polar coordinates $\\left(2\\sqrt{3}k,\\frac{4\\pi}{3}\\right)$, where $k$ is a positive real number. Find its exact Cartesian coordinates in terms of $k$.\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates are\n\n$(${{field-1}}$,$ {{field-2}}$)$.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Use the same conversion formulas, keeping the radius in terms of $k$:\n\n$$\nx=r\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=r\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nSince $k>0$, the radius $2\\sqrt{3}k$ is positive. The angle $\\frac{4\\pi}{3}$ lies in the third quadrant and has reference angle $\\frac{\\pi}{3}$, so\n\n$$\n\\cos\\left(\\frac{4\\pi}{3}\\right)=-\\frac{1}{2},\\qquad\n\\sin\\left(\\frac{4\\pi}{3}\\right)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst calculate the $x$-coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=2\\sqrt{3}k\\cos\\left(\\frac{4\\pi}{3}\\right)\\\\\n &=2\\sqrt{3}k\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\\\\n &=-\\sqrt{3}k.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen calculate the $y$-coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny&=2\\sqrt{3}k\\sin\\left(\\frac{4\\pi}{3}\\right)\\\\\n &=2\\sqrt{3}k\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)\\\\\n &=-3k.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates are $\\left(-\\sqrt{3}k,-3k\\right)$. Both coordinates are negative for every allowed $k$, as expected in the third quadrant."
    }
